<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#antillean-creole">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antillean Creole</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kwéyòl, Patwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antillean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> is a French-based creole language spoken in the Lesser Antilles, including Martinique, Guadeloupe, Dominica and Saint Lucia, where it is called Kwéyòl. It arose among enslaved Africans and French colonists on the plantations of the 17th and 18th centuries, combining mainly French vocabulary with African and Carib influences. In Dominica and Saint Lucia it survived long British rule as the everyday language of most people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antillean-creole.jpg" alt="Antillean Creole" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skimel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signs_in_creole_in_Martinique_-_Dlo_Koko,_Soley,_Lanm%C3%A8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
